gment”). Cognate to Low German Zeddel, Palatine Rhine Franconian Zeddel, English schedule.\nThe spelling with -tt- was found from the 15th century in Upper German; the spelling with -dd- persisted until the 19th century, primarily in authors from Central or Northern Germany. Luther mostly writes zedel, Goethe alternates between zeddel and zettel. The (now obsolete) spelling variant zettul is influenced by French cédule.\nOccasional weak inflection was found in Middle High German and persisted into the 18th century. The original feminine gender was retained until Luther's time, but occasional masculine or neuter gender was found by the late Middle High German period. The masculine gender has predominated since the 19th century.",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[44649,"zettel","noun","a small or loose piece of paper, slip",0,"m"],[44650,"zettel","noun","note, message, letter",1,"m"],[44651,"zettel","noun","poster, placard, public notice",2,"m"],[44652,"zettel","noun","warp",3,"m"],[44653,"zettel","verb","inflection of zetteln:",4],[44654,"zettel","verb","first-person singular present",5],[44655,"zettel","verb","singular imperative",6]],"related_forms":[[45404,"zettel","beipackzettel","derived"],[45405,"zettel","denkzettel","derived"],[45406,"zettel","handzettel","derived"],[45407,"zettel","kassenzettel","derived"],[45408,"zettel","klebezettel","derived"],[45409,"zettel","lohnzettel","derived"],[45410,"zettel","lottozettel","derived"],[45411,"zettel","meldezettel","derived"],[45412,"zettel","merkzettel","derived"],[45413,"zettel","notizzettel","derived"],[45414,"zettel","packzettel","derived"],[45415,"zettel","schmierzettel","derived"],[45416,"zettel","schummelzettel","derived"],[45417,"zettel","spickzettel","derived"],[45418,"zettel","totenzettel","derived"],[45419,"zettel","wahlzettel","derived"],[45420,"zettel","waschzettel","derived"],[45421,"zettel","wunschzettel","derived"],[45422,"zettel","zettelkasten","derived"],[45423,"zettel","zettelwirtschaft","der